<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">State of Jiang</h1><p class="st-meta">1046 BCE – 623 BCE (approx.) · capital Jiang</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Ying-clan state on the upper Huai River that allied with Qi against Chu and was destroyed by Chu in 623 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Jiang was ruled by the Ying clan, which claimed descent from Boyi, an adviser of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yu-the-great">Yu the Great</a>. It lay near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhengyang-county">Zhengyang</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a>, among several small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huai-river">Huai River</a> states recognised by the Zhou.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Almost nothing is recorded of its government. Jiang appears in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zuo-zhuan">Zuo zhuan</a> when, with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-huang">State of Huang</a>, it joined the covenant led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-huan-of-qi">Duke Huan of Qi</a> in the 650s BCE, a gesture of defiance towards the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/state-of-chu">State of Chu</a>, its powerful southern neighbour.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Once Qi's protection faded, Chu took revenge. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-mu-of-chu">King Mu of Chu</a> destroyed Jiang in 623 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-mu-of-qin">Duke Mu of Qin</a> was said to have worn mourning for it. The surname Jiang is traditionally traced to its people.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1046 BCE – 623 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:38.89%;width:8.42%"></span></div>